  'vt.ineq.short': '< > show bigger and smaller', 'vt.antiknight.short': 'no repeats a knight’s move apart', 'vt.kropki.short': 'white dots mean consecutive', 'vt.thermo.short': 'rising along thermometers', 'vt.arrow.short': 'circle = arrow sum',
  'vr.ineq': 'Normal rules, plus a sign (< or >) between two neighboring cells points to the smaller digit. The open side faces the bigger digit.',
  'vr.antiknight': 'Normal rules, plus cells a chess knight’s move apart (two steps, then one to the side) can never hold the same digit.',
  'vr.kropki': 'Normal rules, plus a white dot between two neighboring cells means their digits are consecutive (they differ by 1). Every such pair is marked, so neighbors without a dot are never consecutive.',
  'vr.thermo': 'Normal rules, plus along each thermometer the digits strictly increase from the round bulb to the far end.',
  'vr.arrow': 'Normal rules, plus the digit in a circle equals the sum of the digits along its arrow. Digits on an arrow may repeat unless they share a row, column or box.',
  'vrd.ineq': '<h4>Tip</h4><ul><li>A cell that is bigger than all its neighbors needs a big digit; one that is smaller than all of them needs a small digit.</li><li>In a chain of three &lt; signs, the smallest cell is at most 6 and the biggest is at least 4.</li></ul>',
  'vrd.antiknight': '<h4>Tip</h4><p>Once you place a digit, it can’t appear in any of the (up to) 8 cells a knight’s move away. That trims the candidates in nearby boxes a lot.</p>',
  'vrd.kropki': '<h4>Tip</h4><ul><li>Cells joined by a white dot differ by exactly 1 — next to a 5, only a 4 or a 6 fits.</li><li>A missing dot is a clue too: neighbors without a dot can never be consecutive.</li></ul>',
  'vrd.thermo': '<h4>Tip</h4><ul><li>A thermometer’s length is a clue. On a 5-cell thermometer, the bulb is 1–5 and the tip is 5–9.</li><li>Cells near the bulb hold small digits; cells near the tip hold big ones.</li></ul>',
  'vrd.arrow': '<h4>Tip</h4><ul><li>A circle holds at most 9, so long arrows can only contain small digits.</li><li>Compare the circle’s candidates with the range the arrow can add up to, and remove the impossible ones.</li></ul>',
  'tech.bounds': 'Order bounds', 'tech.dots': 'Consecutive dots', 'tech.arrowSum': 'Arrow sum',
  'x.bounds': 'The signs or the thermometer’s order limit which digits this cell can hold, so candidates outside that range can be removed.',
  'x.dots': 'Neighbors with a white dot must be consecutive, and neighbors without one never are. These links remove impossible candidates.',
  'x.arrowSum': 'The circle must equal the sum along its arrow, so candidates that can’t make that sum work are removed.'
